A field written unquoted in the output may be named like an alias in scope,
which the syntax does not allow, as a quoted label never shadows anything.

-- in.cue --
// The quoted label is written unquoted, named like the alias of x, and a
// field may not be named like an alias in scope, so the alias is renamed.
t1: {
	x~(X): 1
	r:     X
	a: "X": c: X
}

t2: {
	x~(X): 1
	r:     X
	a: "X": 2
}

// The field is in the same scope as the alias, declared after it, so it takes
// the alias's place in that scope; the reference in c reaches x through the
// alias as the field written unquoted shadows x.
t3: {
	x~(X): 1
	"X":   2
	r:     X
	a: "x": c: x
}
-- out/definition --
cannot have both alias and field with name "X" in same scope
// The quoted label is written unquoted, named like the alias of x, and a
// field may not be named like an alias in scope, so the alias is renamed.
t1: {
	x~(X): 1
	r:     X
	a: X: c: X
}
t2: {
	x~(X): 1
	r:     X
	a: X: 2
}

// The field is in the same scope as the alias, declared after it, so it takes
// the alias's place in that scope; the reference in c reaches x through the
// alias as the field written unquoted shadows x.
t3: {
	x~(X): 1
	X:     2
	r:     X
	a: x: c: x
}
-- out/doc --
[]
[t1]
- The quoted label is written unquoted, named like the alias of x, and a
field may not be named like an alias in scope, so the alias is renamed.

[t1 x]
[t1 r]
[t1 a]
[t1 a X]
[t1 a X c]
[t2]
[t2 x]
[t2 r]
[t2 a]
[t2 a X]
[t3]
- The field is in the same scope as the alias, declared after it, so it takes
the alias's place in that scope; the reference in c reaches x through the
alias as the field written unquoted shadows x.

[t3 x]
[t3 X]
[t3 r]
[t3 a]
[t3 a x]
[t3 a x c]
